{"profiles":[{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Gray Bamboo Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Eastern lesser bamboo lemur","source":"Wikipedia"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Hapalemur griseus griseus","confidence":98,"rank":"subspecies","scientificName":"Hapalemur griseus griseus (Link, 1795)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":7261995},"ottId":97009,"rank":"subspecies","scientificName":"Hapalemur griseus griseus","synonyms":["Hapalemur olivaceus","Hapalemur schlegeli","Lemur cinereus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The eastern lesser bamboo lemur (Hapalemur griseus), also known as the gray bamboo lemur, the gray gentle lemur, and the Mahajanga lemur is a small lemur endemic to Madagascar, with three known subspecies. As its name suggests, the eastern lesser bamboo lemur feeds mainly on bamboo.","revisionId":1337116130,"title":"Eastern lesser bamboo lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_lesser_bamboo_lemur","wikidataId":"Q307085"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Ducks","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Geese","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Swans","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Screamers","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Waterfowl","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Screamers, Ducks, Geese, Swans","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Anseriformes","confidence":94,"rank":"order","scientificName":"Anseriformes","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":1108},"ottId":241841,"rank":"order","scientificName":"Anseriformes","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Anseriformes is an order of birds also known as waterfowl that comprises 178 living species of birds in three families: Anhimidae (three species of screamers), Anseranatidae (the magpie goose), and Anatidae, the largest family, which includes the other 174 species of waterfowl, among them the ducks, geese, and swans. Most modern species in the order are highly adapted for an aquatic existence at the water surface.","revisionId":1354912139,"title":"Anseriformes","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anseriformes","wikidataId":"Q21651"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Nunlets","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Nonnula","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Nonnula P.L.Sclater, 1854","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2475120},"ottId":308593,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Nonnula","synonyms":["Nonnulla"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Nonnula is a genus of puffbird in the Bucconidae family. They are commonly known as nunlets.","revisionId":1283364503,"title":"Nonnula","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonnula","wikidataId":"Q2468601"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Buttonquail","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bustardquail","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Button-quail","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Buttonquails","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bustard-quail","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Typical Buttonquail","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Turnix","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Turnix Bonnaterre, 1791","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2474964},"ottId":365489,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Turnix","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Buttonquail or hemipodes are members of a small family of ground birds, Turnicidae, in the order Charadriiformes. Despite their name and resemblance, they bear no taxonomic relationship to Old World quail of the tribe Coturnicini under the order Galliformes, including the species often called \"button quail\".","revisionId":1362512979,"title":"Buttonquail","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buttonquail","wikidataId":"Q205320"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"nematodes","source":"WRiMS"},{"language":"en","name":"Nematode","source":"Checklist of Vermont Specie